WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.592
Before writing more code, think about what Python is doing for us as analysts.

00:00:05.592 --> 00:00:12.722
Python lets us describe a repeatable process once and then reuse it whenever new data arrives.

00:00:12.722 --> 00:00:19.232
A simple analytics workflow often looks like this: represent information with variables,

00:00:19.232 --> 00:00:25.022
transform those values with calculations, and communicate the result with readable output.

00:00:25.545 --> 00:00:28.432
Suppose we begin with sales and costs.

00:00:28.432 --> 00:00:34.187
Those are raw values. We subtract costs from sales to create profit.

00:00:34.187 --> 00:00:39.182
Profit is a derived metric because we created it from other values.

00:00:39.182 --> 00:00:44.827
Then we display the result so another person can understand it.

00:00:44.827 --> 00:00:51.733
That pattern—raw value, code, derived metric, readable result—comes up again and again in analytics.
